ideas.human_approval:
The Owner, or Finance up to a set RM limit (for example RM1,000), approves any release that R1 did not verify. Possible decisions:
- (a) 'Release on credit terms' for a trusted customer, which creates a receivable record.
- (b) 'Match manually' to a credit the owner can see in the bank app but that is not yet in an upload.
- (c) 'Reject and tell sales'.

The decision screen shows:
- the order and the RM at risk;
- the release method ('third-party lorry, 15:00');
- customer history ('first order, 0 past payments');
- the claim screenshot beside the search result ('no credit RM4,850-4,870 from 11:00 to 14:45');
- the rule that fired, and the three nearest credits by amount and time.

R5 cases cannot be overridden, only held or cancelled. Every override needs a reason picked from a list plus free text, and the reason is logged.

ideas.prelim_demo_scope:
Build the release lock end to end, live:
- an order list with claim screenshots;
- a 'simulate bank credit' panel plus a synthetic CSV upload;
- rules R1-R5;
- a role switcher showing the storekeeper's red/green release button;
- the owner's override screen;
- the append-only activity log.

Three-minute script:
1. SJ-1043 turns green after the CSV upload.
2. SJ-1045 shows 'SHORT RM311.99' (the 1-sen trick).
3. SJ-1047, the syndicate-style order, stays locked while the 'lorry' waits, and the owner rejects it.
4. The log replays every step.

Leave out: real bank feeds, the WhatsApp API and multi-branch.

ideas.outcome_and_log:
What users see:
- Morning card: 'Semalam: 47 tuntutan RM3,846.40 | 43 sepadan RM3,109.40 | 4 isu RM737.00 (tiada kredit RM640 dari Liza Agent; resit diguna semula RM45; bukan bayaran - DuitNow Request RM52) | 1 kredit tanpa tuntutan RM25'.
- The owner approves 3 follow-up drafts and flags 'Liza (agent)' as verify-first.
- The next day the RM52 customer pays (an honest mistake), and the row auto-updates to 'Recovered'.
- A weekly view shows RM recovered, RM written off, average hours from sale to detection, and repeat names.

What the activity log records:
- each claim ingested (source, staff member, image hash);
- each AI extraction (model version, fields, confidence);
- each rule outcome;
- each human decision (approve, edit or reject, with before and after values);
- each message sent (text, recipient, time, sender);
- flag changes, the day close (who and when), and recovery events.
The log is append-only and exportable.

ideas.prelim_demo_scope:
Build AI proof reading plus the review screen:
- about 30 synthetic screenshots in several fictional bank and e-wallet layouts, in BM, English and Chinese. Include a request-for-money screen, a 1-sen edit, a reused image and a wrong-recipient QR.
- one synthetic bank CSV;
- a vision-model call that returns claim JSON;
- the rule matcher;
- the side-by-side review screen;
- one BM follow-up draft approved by the owner;
- the activity log and the morning card.
Cache the extraction results so the live demo survives a model or network failure.
